===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
The pictogram of a pair of elephant tusks. According to Xu Shen, this pictogram was used to indicate human molars but not front teeth/incisors (齒).

===Etymology===
{{zh-ref|Norman and Mei (1976)}} propose that this was a substrate loan; cf. {{cog|mkh-vie-pro|*ŋaː||[[ivory]]}} ({{cog|vi|ngà}}), {{cog|tai-pro|*ŋaːᴬ||[[tusk]]; [[ivory]]}} ({{cog|th|งา}}). {{zh-ref|Pulleyblank (1983)}} disagrees with their hypothesis and considers [[Old Chinese]] to be the donor of this [[Wanderwort]] instead.

STEDT provisionally sets up {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*s-ŋja||tusk; tooth}}, comparing it to {{cog|lus|ngho||tusk; fang}}, {{cog|mni|ꯌꯥ|t=tooth}}, {{cog|mro|tr=hngou|t=tooth}}, {{cog|blk|tr=tə́ʔ '''ŋà'''|t=tooth}}.

; Hong Kong Cantonese neologism {{n-g|prefix}}
: From {{zh-l|阿|tr=aa3}}.

